<Wix xmlns="http://wixtoolset.org/schemas/v4/wxs" xmlns:ui="http://wixtoolset.org/schemas/v4/wxs/ui" xmlns:util="http://wixtoolset.org/schemas/v4/wxs/util">
  <Package Name="Morphic" Manufacturer="Raising the Floor - US, Inc." Version="!(bind.FileVersion.morphic_exe)" UpgradeCode="7d0ee114-79e8-4ff6-b28b-0408551483f5" InstallerVersion="500">

    <Icon Id="MorphicIcon" SourceFile="..\Morphic\Assets\Icons\morphic.ico" />
    <Property Id="ARPPRODUCTICON" Value="MorphicIcon" />

    <!-- AllowSameVersionUpgrades="yes" because Morphic puts its build number in the
         4th component of the W.X.Y.Z version, and MSI's version comparison ignores
         the 4th component. Without this attribute, build-over-build upgrades on the
         same Y are blocked with "already installed." -->
    <MajorUpgrade AllowSameVersionUpgrades="yes" DowngradeErrorMessage="!(loc.DowngradeError)" />

    <!-- Close any running Morphic.exe before install. Without this, in-use DLLs can't be
         deleted during the old product's uninstall (Windows schedules them for delete-on-reboot),
         which makes the new install think the files are still present and apply file-version
         rules to them. We send WM_ENDSESSION (EndSessionMessage, default yes) instead of WM_CLOSE
         because MorphicBarWindow intercepts WM_CLOSE -> Hide and would not actually close.
         TerminateProcess="5" force-kills if Morphic doesn't exit within 5 seconds.
         Docs: https://wixtoolset.org/docs/reference/schema/util/closeapplication/ -->
    <util:CloseApplication
        Id="CloseMorphic"
        Target="Morphic.exe"
        CloseMessage="no"
        EndSessionMessage="yes"
        RebootPrompt="no"
        TerminateProcess="5" />

    <MediaTemplate EmbedCab="yes" />

    <!-- Chain WindowsAppRuntimeInstall.exe during install so the Windows App Runtime MSIX
         packages (Framework, Main, Singleton, DDLM) are registered with the OS before
         Morphic first launches. Morphic is framework-dependent on the Windows App Runtime;
         in particular AppNotificationManager has a hard runtime dependency on the
         Singleton package, which cannot be satisfied by any app-local files (see
         Microsoft's self-contained deployment doc). The installer EXE is itself installed
         into INSTALLFOLDER\prerequisites\ by WindowsAppRuntimeInstallerComponent below, so
         by the time these custom actions fire (after InstallFiles), it's on disk and
         runnable.

         We run it via util:WixQuietExec64 rather than a plain FileRef-based CustomAction
         because the FileRef variant creates the child process with a normal console window
         (briefly visible during MSI install; the simple quiet-mode CLI argument suppresses
         the installer's own UI but not the console host that wraps the process).
         WixQuietExec64 creates the process with CREATE_NO_WINDOW so no console flashes on
         screen, AND it captures stdout/stderr into the MSI install log for debugging if
         the chained install ever fails.

         TWO-CA pattern (Morphic is installed for all users into Program Files, so we need
         the runtime registered for all users too, including the one currently running the
         installer):

           1. RunWindowsAppRuntimeInstallerForAllUsers (deferred, Impersonate="no") runs
              the EXE as NT AUTHORITY\SYSTEM. MSIX deployment in the SYSTEM context calls
              ProvisionPackageForAllUsersAsync, which STAGES the packages on disk and
              PROVISIONS them for all users, but registration into each user profile is
              deferred until that user's next logon. So this CA succeeds quickly (~2s
              because the SYSTEM context already had the packages from prior installs in
              our dev cycle) and makes the runtime available to all future logins as well
              as any existing users on next logon, but the CURRENTLY-logged-in user's
              session has no registered packages yet.
           2. RunWindowsAppRuntimeInstallerForCurrentUser (deferred, Impersonate="yes")
              runs the EXE as the logged-in user (with elevation inherited from the MSI's
              UAC consent). MSIX deployment in user context finishes the deferred
              registration immediately for that user, so Morphic.exe launched right after
              the installer finds the runtime ready in their session.

         The two CAs run the same EXE with the same arguments; what differs is impersonation.
         The user-context invocation is cheap because the SYSTEM action already staged the
         packages: it's effectively a per-user link/register step.

         Pattern detail (for each CA): A "Set..." immediate CA writes the full command line
         into a property whose name matches the deferred CA; MSI passes that property
         value through to the deferred CA as its CustomActionData. The deferred CA reads
         CustomActionData and runs WixQuietExec64. [#WindowsAppRuntimeInstall_exe] is the
         WiX install-time path-of-file token; it resolves to the full installed path of
         WindowsAppRuntimeInstall.exe in INSTALLFOLDER\prerequisites\ at install time.

         Return="ignore" on both deferred CAs means a runtime-install failure (network
         hiccup, partial machine state, etc.) does NOT abort the Morphic install: the app
         still installs and starts, AppNotificationManager.IsSupported() returns false at
         runtime, and ToastNotifications.Initialize silently skips registration. Failing
         the whole install over a non-critical sub-installer would be worse UX than
         degrading the toast feature. Condition NOT REMOVE skips both CAs on uninstall
         (the file is being removed anyway, and we don't want to touch the system Windows
         App Runtime when the user uninstalls Morphic). -->
    <CustomAction
        Id="SetRunWindowsAppRuntimeInstallerForAllUsers"
        Property="RunWindowsAppRuntimeInstallerForAllUsers"
        Value="&quot;[#WindowsAppRuntimeInstall_exe]&quot; --quiet"
        Execute="immediate" />

    <CustomAction
        Id="RunWindowsAppRuntimeInstallerForAllUsers"
        BinaryRef="Wix4UtilCA_X64"
        DllEntry="WixQuietExec64"
        Execute="deferred"
        Return="ignore"
        Impersonate="no" />

    <CustomAction
        Id="SetRunWindowsAppRuntimeInstallerForCurrentUser"
        Property="RunWindowsAppRuntimeInstallerForCurrentUser"
        Value="&quot;[#WindowsAppRuntimeInstall_exe]&quot; --quiet"
        Execute="immediate" />

    <CustomAction
        Id="RunWindowsAppRuntimeInstallerForCurrentUser"
        BinaryRef="Wix4UtilCA_X64"
        DllEntry="WixQuietExec64"
        Execute="deferred"
        Return="ignore"
        Impersonate="yes" />

    <!-- Run `Morphic.NotificationHelper.exe` with the unregister flag during uninstall to
         remove the COM activator's HKCU registrations. The helper's Unregister() call cleans BOTH
         the AppUserModelId\<aumid>\Activator tree AND the matching CLSID\{generated}\
         LocalServer32 tree (the latter we cannot pre-create in WiX because the CLSID is
         SDK-generated and not known at install time).

         Mirrors the SetRunWindowsAppRuntimeInstallerFor*** /
         RunWindowsAppRuntimeInstallerFor*** pattern above: an immediate CA stages the
         command line into a property whose name matches the deferred CA, then the
         deferred WixQuietExec64 CA reads the property as CustomActionData and runs the
         EXE with CREATE_NO_WINDOW (no console flash visible during uninstall).

         Impersonate="yes" because the registry writes we're cleaning are in HKCU and
         we need to clean the CURRENT USER's HKCU, not the elevated install context's
         HKCU. Limitation: this only cleans the uninstalling user's keys; if other
         users on the machine had also run Morphic and their HKCU still has stale
         registration, those orphans remain (matches the standard MSI per-user-component
         limitation for any HKCU state).

         Sequenced BEFORE RemoveFiles so the helper exe still exists on disk when called.
         Return="ignore" so a stale helper or transient failure does not block the
         uninstall; the AUMID-side registry key is also marked ForceDeleteOnUninstall via
         NotificationHelperAumidRegistryComponent as a backstop covering the most
         user-visible orphan if the CA fails. -->
    <CustomAction
        Id="SetUnregisterNotificationHelper"
        Property="UnregisterNotificationHelper"
        Value="&quot;[#notification_helper_exe]&quot; --unregister"
        Execute="immediate" />

    <CustomAction
        Id="UnregisterNotificationHelper"
        BinaryRef="Wix4UtilCA_X64"
        DllEntry="WixQuietExec64"
        Execute="deferred"
        Return="ignore"
        Impersonate="yes" />

    <!-- DTF-wrapped managed CA assembly produced by the MorphicSetup.Actions project.
         The $(var.MorphicSetup.Actions.TargetDir) preprocessor variable is auto-set by
         WiX from the <ProjectReference> in MorphicSetup.wixproj and points at that
         project's $(OutputPath) (i.e., bin/$(Configuration)/), where MorphicSetup.Actions
         copies the .CA.dll via its CopyManagedCustomActionToOutputPath target. The
         architecture-specific SfxCA stub is selected at build time inside the CA
         project based on $(Platform), so this single Binary reference works for both
         x64 and ARM64 builds without further switching. -->
    <Binary Id="MorphicSetupActionsCA" SourceFile="$(var.MorphicSetup.Actions.TargetDir)MorphicSetup.Actions.CA.dll" />

    <!-- ReadWindowsUbr populates the WINDOWSUBR MSI property so the launch condition
         can do a numeric >= 6456 check against it. Must run AFTER AppSearch (so
         WINDOWSBUILDNUMBER is also populated for the surrounding launch condition
         clauses) and BEFORE LaunchConditions. Scheduled in both InstallUISequence
         (interactive installs) and InstallExecuteSequence (silent installs).

         Impersonate="no": when this CA runs in InstallExecuteSequence on the server
         side, it executes in the elevated MSI service's process tree. The default
         (Impersonate="yes") spawns the SfxCA host as a token-restricted impersonation
         of the invoking user, and SfxCA then tries to extract its embedded assembly
         payload to a temp directory it doesn't have write access to, failing with
         error 5 (ACCESS_DENIED). Running as LocalSystem instead gives the CA host
         unambiguous access to SYSTEM's temp dir; reading HKLM (where UBR lives) and
         writing an MSI property both work fine from SYSTEM context, so the change
         has no other behavioral implications. -->
    <CustomAction Id="ReadWindowsUbr"
                  BinaryRef="MorphicSetupActionsCA"
                  DllEntry="ReadWindowsUbr"
                  Execute="immediate"
                  Impersonate="no"
                  Return="check" />

    <InstallUISequence>
      <!-- NOT Installed: only run during fresh install. Skipping for uninstall, repair,
           and modify, where the launch condition is also bypassed (see <Launch>) and the
           CA has no useful work to do. Without this gate the CA fires during uninstall
           too, and SfxCA's temp-directory extraction fails on the server-side maintenance
           context (basic UI uninstall runs the entire sequence on the server, with no
           preceding client UI run to provide a user-temp path the SfxCA stub can use). -->
      <Custom Action="ReadWindowsUbr" After="AppSearch" Condition="NOT Installed" />
    </InstallUISequence>

    <InstallExecuteSequence>
      <!-- Condition has two parts ANDed together:
             1. NOT Installed: skip during uninstall, repair, and modify; the launch
                condition is also bypassed in those scenarios (see <Launch>) so there
                is no UBR check to support. The basic-UI uninstall path runs entirely
                on the server, and an immediate impersonated CA there hits a SfxCA
                temp-directory extraction failure (error 5 / ACCESS_DENIED) before our
                code runs (a real install log captured this exact failure), so we
                must not let the CA fire during maintenance ops at all.
             2. WINDOWSUBR="0": only run the CA server-side if WINDOWSUBR is still at
                the default value defined on the <Property> above. In an interactive
                fresh install the InstallUISequence ran the CA on the client side and
                the value transferred across via Secure="yes"; running it again on the
                server is pure overhead. In a silent fresh install the UI sequence was
                skipped, so WINDOWSUBR is still "0" and the CA needs to run here. -->
      <Custom Action="ReadWindowsUbr" After="AppSearch" Condition="NOT Installed AND WINDOWSUBR=&quot;0&quot;" />
      <!-- SYSTEM-context provisioning first (stages packages for all users), then
           user-context registration (makes the runtime active for the current session
           immediately so Morphic.exe finds it on first launch). See the comment block on
           the CustomActions above for the full rationale. -->
      <Custom Action="SetRunWindowsAppRuntimeInstallerForAllUsers" Before="RunWindowsAppRuntimeInstallerForAllUsers" Condition="NOT REMOVE" />
      <Custom Action="RunWindowsAppRuntimeInstallerForAllUsers" After="InstallFiles" Condition="NOT REMOVE" />
      <Custom Action="SetRunWindowsAppRuntimeInstallerForCurrentUser" Before="RunWindowsAppRuntimeInstallerForCurrentUser" Condition="NOT REMOVE" />
      <Custom Action="RunWindowsAppRuntimeInstallerForCurrentUser" After="RunWindowsAppRuntimeInstallerForAllUsers" Condition="NOT REMOVE" />
      <!-- Condition: REMOVE="ALL" fires only on pure uninstall (not upgrade-driven
           removes of the old product, which are handled by NOT UPGRADINGPRODUCTCODE).
           During an upgrade we DON'T want to unregister, because the old product's
           uninstall and the new product's install both touch the same HKCU keys and
           the new install's Register call would set them up again immediately; an
           intervening unregister just creates a brief race window. On pure uninstall,
           unregister is exactly what we want. -->
      <Custom Action="SetUnregisterNotificationHelper" Before="UnregisterNotificationHelper" Condition="REMOVE=&quot;ALL&quot; AND NOT UPGRADINGPRODUCTCODE" />
      <Custom Action="UnregisterNotificationHelper" Before="RemoveFiles" Condition="REMOVE=&quot;ALL&quot; AND NOT UPGRADINGPRODUCTCODE" />
      <!-- Launch Morphic.exe after the install is fully committed. Matches 1.x's
           "After InstallFinalize" placement. Conditions: fresh install only (not
           repair, uninstall, or upgrade-removal); MORPHIC_LAUNCH_AFTER_INSTALL
           public property is enabled (IT admin override); LogonUser is populated
           (rules out completely user-less SYSTEM contexts where the
           Impersonate=yes CA has no user session to spawn into); LogonUser is
           not literally "SYSTEM" (the common SCCM/MDM identity). See the
           CustomAction's comment block above for the full rationale and the matrix
           of which install scenarios fire vs skip. -->
      <Custom Action="LaunchMorphicAfterInstall"
              After="InstallFinalize"
              Condition="NOT Installed AND MORPHIC_LAUNCH_AFTER_INSTALL=&quot;1&quot; AND LogonUser AND LogonUser &lt;&gt; &quot;SYSTEM&quot;" />
    </InstallExecuteSequence>

    <Feature Id="Main">
      <ComponentGroupRef Id="MorphicComponents" />
      <ComponentRef Id="ApplicationComponent" />
      <ComponentRef Id="NotificationHelperAumidRegistryComponent" />
      <ComponentRef Id="ActiveSetupForRuntimeRegistrationComponent" />
      <ComponentRef Id="AutoStartRunKeyComponent" />
    </Feature>

    <ui:WixUI Id="WixUI_Minimal" />
    <WixVariable Id="WixUILicenseRtf" Value="License.rtf" />

    <UIRef Id="WixUI_ErrorProgressText" />

    <!-- ProgressText writes to the MSI ActionText table; the installer's progress dialog
         shows this string while the matching custom action is running. Without these, the
         dialog's "Action:" line would just show the raw CustomAction Id (e.g.
         "RunWindowsAppRuntimeInstallerForAllUsers") for ~30 seconds while the chained
         installer silently runs, making the user think the install hung. With these, the
         user sees a human-readable description and knows the install is still progressing.
         [actually, MSI installer exe seems to normally give a generic message] -->
    <UI>
      <ProgressText Action="RunWindowsAppRuntimeInstallerForAllUsers" Message="Installing the Windows App Runtime (this may take a moment)..." />
      <ProgressText Action="RunWindowsAppRuntimeInstallerForCurrentUser" Message="Registering the Windows App Runtime for your account..." />
      <ProgressText Action="UnregisterNotificationHelper" Message="Removing Morphic's notification helper registration..." />
    </UI>
	
    <Property Id="WINDOWSBUILDNUMBER" Secure="yes">
      <RegistrySearch Id="BuildNumberSearch" Root="HKLM" Key="SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows NT\CurrentVersion" Name="CurrentBuildNumber" Type="raw" />
    </Property>

    <!-- UBR (Update Build Revision) is the 4th component of the full Windows build
         number string "10.0.19045.NNNN", stored in HKLM\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows NT\
         CurrentVersion\UBR as a REG_DWORD.

         We previously used the built-in <RegistrySearch Type="raw"> here, which is
         supposed to produce a "#NNNN" integer-tagged property for REG_DWORD values
         per the MSI/WiX docs. Verbose install logs from real machines showed that
         search silently failing to populate the property at all (no PROPERTY CHANGE
         line, property absent from the final property dump) on both Win10 22H2 and
         Win11 24H2 builds. The launch condition then fell through with WINDOWSUBR
         unset, blocking installs on Win10 22H2 even at the latest-free-update level.

         The ReadWindowsUbr custom action below (in MorphicSetup.Actions) reads UBR
         via .NET's Microsoft.Win32.Registry and writes the property explicitly as a
         plain decimal string (NOT the documented "#NNNN" integer-tagged form). A real
         Win10 22H2 install proved that engine's condition evaluator does NOT honor
         the "#" prefix integer convention: "#6456" >= 6456 evaluated as a string
         vs. integer-literal mismatch and the install was blocked even at the UBR
         floor. Storing the property as plain "NNNN" lets the launch condition do a
         string-vs-string comparison against a quoted literal "6456"; lexicographic
         comparison lines up with numeric order for the values that matter (Win10
         22H2 free-channel UBRs are all 4 digits and capped at 6456 since Microsoft
         ended free updates to that OS line).

         The default Value="0" ensures the property is always defined; if the CA
         doesn't run (some MSI internal failure before AppSearch), the launch
         condition's "WINDOWSUBR >= '6456'" clause fails closed rather than producing
         an MSI evaluation error. -->
    <Property Id="WINDOWSUBR" Secure="yes" Value="0" />

    <!-- Launch condition. The OS floor is driven by .NET 10's CoreCLR requiring OS
         exports/behaviors that Microsoft added across cumulative updates between
         Win10 22H2's original release (UBR 2006, Oct 2022) and the final free Win10
         22H2 update (UBR 6456, Oct 2025). On an under-patched Win10 22H2, Morphic
         crashes during CLR EE startup with 0x80131506 BEFORE any managed code runs
         (no remediation possible from our side; the OS must provide what the CLR
         expects).

         Accepted versions:
           1. Win11 (build > 19045): all current builds satisfy .NET 10 requirements.
           2. Win10 22H2 (build = 19045) at UBR >= 6456: the final free Win10 22H2
              update. Anyone on the free-update channel can be at this level.
         Anything else is blocked with a clear "run Windows Update" message.

         The 6456 floor is the safest defensible choice (last free Win10 22H2
         servicing release) but it MIGHT be safe to lower. Empirically we have also
         confirmed Morphic launches cleanly on Win10 22H2 at UBR 4780 (August 2024
         cumulative). We chose 6456 to align with the "fully-patched-on-the-free-tier"
         user story rather than reflect the strict technical minimum. If we later get
         a customer report that their fully-up-to-date-as-of-some-earlier-date Win10
         22H2 install (e.g., UBR somewhere between 4780 and 6456) is now blocked,
         consider lowering this floor to 4780 (or to a value bisected between 2006
         fail and 4780 pass if even finer-grained inclusion matters). The error
         message also needs updating to match any change. -->
    <!-- Note the quoting on the WINDOWSBUILDNUMBER comparisons: WINDOWSBUILDNUMBER is
         populated from the REG_SZ CurrentBuildNumber, so it lands in the MSI property
         table as a string ("19045", "26200", etc.). Per MSI's documented conditional
         statement syntax, comparing a string property to an unquoted integer literal
         is supposed to return FALSE; in practice newer Windows 11 MSI engines do an
         implicit numeric conversion and accept it, but Win10 22H2's MSI engine
         enforces the documented behavior strictly and rejects the comparison. Quoting
         "19045" makes both sides strings and gets lexicographic comparison, which
         matches numeric order for all current and foreseeable Windows build numbers
         (they're all 5 digits starting with a digit >= 1: 19045 / 22000 / 22631 /
         26100 / 26200, etc.).

         WINDOWSUBR uses a quoted string literal "6456" for the same reason: the CA
         writes the property as a plain decimal string (see the comment on the
         WINDOWSUBR <Property> above for why the "#" integer-tag form was abandoned).
         Lexicographic comparison of 4-digit UBR strings matches numeric order across
         the only range we care about (Win10 22H2 free-channel UBRs all 4 digits and
         capped at 6456).

         "Installed OR" prefix: skip the entire OS-version gate when the product is
         already installed, i.e., we're in any maintenance operation (uninstall,
         repair, modify). Without it, uninstall on a system where the CA didn't run
         (the CA itself is gated on NOT Installed) sees WINDOWSUBR at its default "0"
         and the launch condition would block the uninstall with a "min OS" message
         the user can't act on. Fresh install still goes through the OS check because
         Installed is empty on first install. Major-upgrade re-install also still
         goes through it because the new product has a fresh ProductCode and is not
         Installed in its own session. -->
    <Launch Condition="Installed OR (WINDOWSBUILDNUMBER &gt; &quot;19045&quot;) OR (WINDOWSBUILDNUMBER = &quot;19045&quot; AND WINDOWSUBR &gt;= &quot;6456&quot;)" Message="!(loc.MinimumOsError)" />

    <!-- Public MSI property that gates the ActiveSetupForRuntimeRegistrationComponent
         install. Default is "1" (Active Setup enabled), which is the right behavior for
         end-user installs because it ensures the Windows App Runtime Framework is
         registered into every new user's profile at first logon (see the component's
         comment for the full rationale).

         Enterprise IT admins who manage WinAppRuntime deployment through their own
         mechanism (SCCM, GPO, Intune, custom Active Setup at the OU level, packaged
         runtime install via MDM, etc.) can suppress Morphic's Active Setup registration
         at install time by passing the property explicitly:
             msiexec /i MorphicSetup.msi MORPHIC_ACTIVE_SETUP=0 /qn
         When set to anything other than "1", the Active Setup component is skipped;
         Morphic's existing per-user CA still registers the runtime for the installing
         user (so single-user deployments still work), but no per-user re-execution at
         future logons happens.

         Secure="yes" because this is a public property: enterprise installers commonly
         run msiexec from non-interactive contexts (Group Policy, MDM), and the secure
         attribute is required for public-prefix properties to be honored across the
         elevated client-server boundary inside MSI. -->
    <Property Id="MORPHIC_ACTIVE_SETUP" Value="1" Secure="yes" />

    <!-- Public MSI property that gates the post-install LaunchMorphicAfterInstall
         custom action. Default is "1" (Morphic launches after install completes when
         it is safe to do so; see the CustomAction's full condition for what "safe"
         means; short version: there's a real logged-in user, not the SYSTEM account
         that SCCM/MDM uses for unattended deploys).

         Enterprise IT admins running silent installs in user-context contexts who
         STILL don't want the post-install launch (e.g., they want to control launch
         timing themselves) pass this explicitly:
             msiexec /i MorphicSetup.msi MORPHIC_LAUNCH_AFTER_INSTALL=0 /qn

         When set to anything other than "1", the post-install launch is skipped. The
         autostart Run key (governed separately by MORPHIC_AUTOSTART) is unaffected;
         Morphic will still auto-start at the user's NEXT logon unless that property
         is also overridden.

         Secure="yes" for the same reason as MORPHIC_ACTIVE_SETUP above. -->
    <Property Id="MORPHIC_LAUNCH_AFTER_INSTALL" Value="1" Secure="yes" />

    <!-- Public MSI property that gates the AutoStartRunKeyComponent. Default is "1"
         (Morphic auto-starts at every user logon). When set to anything other than "1",
         the HKLM Run key is not written and Morphic only launches when the user
         explicitly clicks the Start Menu shortcut.

         Enterprise IT admins who want to suppress the auto-start (kiosks, lab machines,
         deployments where Morphic should be launched on demand only) pass it explicitly:
             msiexec /i MorphicSetup.msi MORPHIC_AUTOSTART=0 /qn

         Per-user enable/disable AFTER install is handled separately via Windows'
         Task Manager Startup tab (which writes to
         HKCU\Software\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\Explorer\StartupApproved\Run);
         this property only governs whether the HKLM Run entry exists in the first place.

         Secure="yes" for the same reason as MORPHIC_ACTIVE_SETUP above. -->
    <Property Id="MORPHIC_AUTOSTART" Value="1" Secure="yes" />

    <!-- Post-install launch: runs Morphic.exe in InstallExecuteSequence after
         InstallFinalize. Conditions are designed to distinguish "a real user
         triggered this install" (fire the launch) from "this is an unattended
         backend deploy" (skip the launch).

         The naive condition would be UILevel >= 5 (interactive UI only), but that
         excludes silent installs done by real users; e.g., winget runs MSIs at
         UILevel = 2 (silent) but in the user's logged-in session; the Microsoft
         Store likewise runs in user context with no MSI UI; these absolutely
         should launch Morphic when their installs finish (the user clicked
         "install" and expects Morphic to start). Gating on UILevel alone would
         break those cases.

         The right discriminator is LogonUser. MSI populates it with the username
         of the account that initiated the install:
           * winget: user's account (fires the launch; desired)
           * Microsoft Store: user's account (fires; desired)
           * Admin running msiexec /qn from their elevated shell: their account (fires)
           * Interactive end-user installer GUI: user's account (fires)
           * SCCM/Intune/MDM in SYSTEM context: "SYSTEM" or empty (skips; desired)
         The condition checks LogonUser is non-empty AND not "SYSTEM"; that
         captures every real-user install path including silent ones.

         There's also the MORPHIC_LAUNCH_AFTER_INSTALL public property (default 1)
         as an explicit override: IT admins running silent installs in user-context
         scenarios who want to suppress the launch anyway can pass
         MORPHIC_LAUNCH_AFTER_INSTALL=0 on the msiexec command line.

         The launch passes the run-after-install flag so the post-install launch respects the user's
         persisted MorphicBar visibility instead of force-showing (today the same treatment as the
         logon autostart's run-after-login, but a separate flag so install behavior can diverge
         from login later). This keeps a bar the user had hidden from reappearing after an update.
         (A manual Start Menu / .exe launch passes no flag and force-shows.)

         We use a file-based exe CustomAction (FileRef + ExeCommand) rather than the util WixShellExec
         helper because WixShellExec's target is a path only and cannot carry the run-after-install
         argument. With FileRef, ExeCommand is the ARGUMENTS ONLY (not the exe
         path). Return="asyncNoWait" launches Morphic and returns immediately (Morphic does its own
         init in the background), so the installer doesn't stall, and unlike QtExec it does not
         wait for the long-running process to exit. Execute="immediate" + Impersonate="yes" run
         Morphic in the installing user's session with their token, not as SYSTEM. (FileRef also lets
         us drop the old Wix4UtilCA_$(sys.BUILDARCHSHORT) arch-matching syntax entirely.) -->
    <CustomAction Id="LaunchMorphicAfterInstall"
                  FileRef="morphic_exe"
                  ExeCommand="--run-after-install"
                  Execute="immediate"
                  Impersonate="yes"
                  Return="asyncNoWait" />
  </Package>

  <Fragment>
    <StandardDirectory Id="ProgramFiles6432Folder">
      <Directory Id="INSTALLFOLDER" Name="!(bind.Property.ProductName)" />
    </StandardDirectory>
    
    <!-- ApplicationComponent: per-user component (HKCU KeyPath) that installs the Start Menu
         shortcut and the "installed=1" registry marker (the latter doubles as the KeyPath that
         ICE43 requires for any component containing a non-advertised shortcut). The shortcut's
         Target=[#morphic_exe] references a file in a DIFFERENT component (MorphicComponents,
         below); this is Microsoft's documented pattern for non-advertised shortcuts and
         intentionally triggers ICE69, which is suppressed in MorphicSetup.wixproj. Putting the
         shortcut in the same component as the file instead breaks both ICE43 (file as KeyPath
         when component has a non-advertised shortcut) and ICE57 (per-user shortcut data with
         per-machine file KeyPath). -->
    <StandardDirectory Id="ProgramMenuFolder">
      <Component Id="ApplicationComponent" Guid="8BDA9AF8-9BCC-4B25-8BCF-EE06A3412358">
        <Shortcut
          Id="MorphicStartMenuShortcut"
          Name="!(bind.Property.ProductName)"
          Icon="MorphicIcon"
          Target="[#morphic_exe]"
          WorkingDirectory="INSTALLFOLDER">
          <!-- System.AppUserModel.ID stamps this shortcut with Morphic's AUMID so that any
               launch from the Start Menu/taskbar (or a pinned copy of this shortcut) tells
               the Shell upfront which AUMID the process belongs to. Without this property the
               Shell infers an AUMID from the .exe path (a hash that differs from what the
               running process sets via SetCurrentProcessExplicitAppUserModelID), which causes
               toast notifications to be misattributed, taskbar grouping to fragment, and
               Start Menu pin tracking to lose continuity across reinstalls.

               The value MUST match Morphic.WindowsNative.AppIdentity.AumidHelper.AppUserModelId.
               If that constant ever changes, update this line in the same commit; mismatched
               values reintroduce the same Shell-attribution problems this property exists to
               prevent. Docs: https://wixtoolset.org/docs/reference/schema/wxs/shortcutproperty/ -->
          <ShortcutProperty Key="System.AppUserModel.ID" Value="RaisingTheFloor.Morphic" />
        </Shortcut>
        <RegistryValue Root="HKCU" Key="Software\Raising the Floor\Morphic" Name="installed" Type="integer" Value="1" KeyPath="yes" />
      </Component>

      <!-- Backstop cleanup for the HKCU AUMID-side registration tree. The
           UnregisterNotificationHelper custom action above is the primary cleanup
           (calls AppNotificationManager.Default.Unregister() which removes both this
           key AND the CLSID-side key), but if that CA fails for any reason (helper
           missing on disk, transient error), this WiX-level deletion still cleans the
           most user-visible orphan. The CLSID-side key cannot be similarly backstopped
           because its GUID is auto-generated by the SDK and not known at install time.

           ForceDeleteOnUninstall removes the key + all nested values + subkeys, so even
           though the SDK's Register() call adds its own values inside this key at
           runtime (Activator\CLSID, etc.), the uninstall sweeps them all.

           Per-user component (HKCU KeyPath via RegistryValue); MSI per-user component
           lifecycle handles per-user install/uninstall automatically. The RegistryValue
           is a placeholder marker that gives the component a KeyPath; the runtime
           Register() writes go into other names within the same key and don't conflict.

           Lives under ProgramMenuFolder as a sibling to ApplicationComponent for
           consistency (both are per-user HKCU-keyed components). The Directory parent
           does not affect HKCU-based components functionally; ProgramMenuFolder is just
           a scope chosen to match. -->
      <Component Id="NotificationHelperAumidRegistryComponent" Guid="9F3C1E5B-7A2D-4F8B-9C6E-5D4A3B2C1F0E">
        <RegistryKey Root="HKCU"
                     Key="Software\Classes\AppUserModelId\RaisingTheFloor.Morphic"
                     ForceDeleteOnUninstall="yes">
          <RegistryValue Type="string" Name="MsiInstallMarker" Value="1" KeyPath="yes" />
        </RegistryKey>
      </Component>
    </StandardDirectory>
  </Fragment>

  <Fragment>
    <!-- Subdirectory of INSTALLFOLDER (Program Files\Morphic\prerequisites\) that holds the
         chained WindowsAppRuntimeInstall.exe. Kept in its own folder (instead of dropping
         the EXE at the install root) to avoid confusing users who browse Program Files\Morphic
         and would not expect to see a second installer EXE alongside Morphic.exe. Leaving
         the file in place after install (rather than deleting it post-CA) lets future
         repair/upgrade scenarios re-run the runtime installer without re-downloading.
         DirectoryRef threads onto the existing INSTALLFOLDER definition (above) so we add
         a child without redefining the parent. -->
    <DirectoryRef Id="INSTALLFOLDER">
      <Directory Id="PrerequisitesFolder" Name="prerequisites">
        <!-- Single component (single key file), so no explicit Guid needed; WiX will
             auto-generate a stable one from the install path. The component is referenced
             from the MorphicComponents ComponentGroup below so it ships in the Main feature
             alongside the rest of Morphic. -->
        <Component Id="WindowsAppRuntimeInstallerComponent">
          <!-- Name="WindowsAppRuntimeInstall.exe" gives the installed file a uniform name
               independent of architecture, so the CustomAction's FileRef resolves the same
               way regardless of which architecture-specific source EXE was built in. -->
          <?if $(sys.BUILDARCH) = "x64" ?>
            <File Id="WindowsAppRuntimeInstall_exe" Source="prerequisites\WindowsAppRuntimeInstall-x64.exe" Name="WindowsAppRuntimeInstall.exe" KeyPath="yes" />
          <?elseif $(sys.BUILDARCH) = "arm64" ?>
            <File Id="WindowsAppRuntimeInstall_exe" Source="prerequisites\WindowsAppRuntimeInstall-arm64.exe" Name="WindowsAppRuntimeInstall.exe" KeyPath="yes" />
          <?endif?>
        </Component>

        <!-- ActiveSetupForRuntimeRegistrationComponent: per-machine HKLM registration
             that triggers a per-user invocation of WindowsAppRuntimeInstall.exe at each
             user's logon. Companion to the SYSTEM+user two-CA pattern above.

             Why this exists: our SYSTEM-context CA
             (RunWindowsAppRuntimeInstallerForAllUsers) calls into MSIX deployment which
             provisions Main, Singleton, and DDLM packages for all users, but it does
             NOT provision the Framework package (Microsoft.WindowsAppRuntime.2). The
             Framework only gets registered into a user's profile via a user-context
             invocation. Our second CA (RunWindowsAppRuntimeInstallerForCurrentUser,
             Impersonate=yes) handles the user who ran the installer. For ANY OTHER user
             who later logs into the same machine, neither CA fires for them; they would
             hit Morphic's bootstrap "Required components missing" dialog on first launch
             without this Active Setup entry.

             How Active Setup works: at each user logon, Windows enumerates HKLM\...\
             Active Setup\Installed Components, and for each GUID checks the per-user
             HKCU mirror. If HKLM\...\{GUID}\Version > HKCU\...\{GUID}\Version (or the
             HKCU subkey doesn't exist), Windows synchronously runs the HKLM StubPath in
             the user's session and copies the Version into HKCU on success. Effect for
             our case: every new user gets WindowsAppRuntimeInstall.exe (quiet mode) run once
             in their session at first logon, which registers the Framework into their
             profile.

             Version format note: Active Setup compares Version values with comma
             separators, not dots. The value here ("2,1,3,0") deliberately tracks the
             bundled WinAppSDK version. WHEN BUMPING WinAppSDK to a newer release,
             update this Version string in the same commit, so existing users will then
             pick up a re-run at their next logon and re-register against the new
             runtime. (Leaving the version stale is harmless on a stable WinAppSDK; it
             just means users only run StubPath once ever.)

             The Active Setup subkey GUID and the Component Guid are intentionally the
             same value, which makes registry inspection easier (the subkey directly
             matches the component). The default registry value also serves as the
             KeyPath so MSI tracks this component via the registry presence.

             Per-machine component (HKLM keypath); MSI uninstall removes the HKLM key
             cleanly. Per-user HKCU mirrors that Active Setup writes during normal
             operation are NOT removed by MSI uninstall (each user owns their own
             HKCU); harmless orphans that the next install would overwrite anyway. -->
        <Component Id="ActiveSetupForRuntimeRegistrationComponent"
                   Guid="7C8B9A6F-3D2E-4B1A-9F8E-5C7D6E8A2B3F"
                   Condition="MORPHIC_ACTIVE_SETUP=&quot;1&quot;">
          <RegistryKey Root="HKLM"
                       Key="SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Active Setup\Installed Components\{7C8B9A6F-3D2E-4B1A-9F8E-5C7D6E8A2B3F}">
            <RegistryValue Type="string"
                           Value="Morphic Windows App Runtime per-user registration"
                           KeyPath="yes" />
            <RegistryValue Name="Version" Type="string" Value="2,1,3,0" />
            <RegistryValue Name="StubPath" Type="string"
                           Value="&quot;[#WindowsAppRuntimeInstall_exe]&quot; --quiet" />
          </RegistryKey>
        </Component>

        <!-- AutoStartRunKeyComponent: writes a per-machine HKLM Run entry so Morphic
             auto-starts at every user's logon on this machine. As an accessibility
             tool installed for all users, the per-machine HKLM Run is the natural
             default (every user benefits without each having to enable it themselves).

             Per-user opt-out without admin: each user can disable Morphic's auto-start
             from Task Manager's Startup tab (or Settings > Apps > Startup). Both UIs
             write a per-user suppression marker at
             HKCU\Software\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\Explorer\StartupApproved\Run\Morphic
             which Windows checks at logon and respects without touching our HKLM key.

             Enterprise opt-out at install time: the MORPHIC_AUTOSTART public property
             gates this component. Default 1 (component installs); set to anything else
             via msiexec command line to skip the Run-key write entirely.

             Ordering at logon: Run keys are processed by Explorer.exe AFTER Active
             Setup completes synchronously, so for new users the WinAppRuntime Framework
             is registered (by the Active Setup StubPath above) before Morphic.exe
             launches via the Run key. No bootstrap-dialog race.

             Value-name "Morphic" matches the friendly name shown in Task Manager's
             Startup tab; users who want to disable will find it under that label.
             The value data is the full quoted EXE path followed by the run-after-login
             CLI flag (the post-install launch passes a separate run-after-install flag;
             the app reads either at startup via Environment.GetCommandLineArgs and treats
             its presence as "an automatic start, so respect the persisted bar visibility" vs
             absence as "user launched me manually, force-show the bar"). The quoting is
             defensive against future install-path changes (Program Files has a space). -->
        <Component Id="AutoStartRunKeyComponent"
                   Guid="D8E5B4A7-2C1F-4F9E-B6D3-7A8C9E1F2B5D"
                   Condition="MORPHIC_AUTOSTART=&quot;1&quot;">
          <RegistryKey Root="HKLM"
                       Key="SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\Run">
            <RegistryValue Name="Morphic"
                           Type="string"
                           Value="&quot;[#morphic_exe]&quot; --run-after-login"
                           KeyPath="yes" />
          </RegistryKey>
        </Component>
      </Directory>
    </DirectoryRef>
  </Fragment>

  <Fragment>
    <ComponentGroup Id="MorphicComponents" Directory="INSTALLFOLDER">
      <!-- Pull the runtime installer Component into the main feature so it ships with
           Morphic and the CustomAction in Package.wxs can resolve WindowsAppRuntimeInstall_exe. -->
      <ComponentRef Id="WindowsAppRuntimeInstallerComponent" />
      <!-- morphic.exe's Component also contains Morphic.Core.dll and Microsoft.DiaSymReader.Native.amd64.dll
           as CompanionFile entries. The previous installer (v1.x) shipped these two DLLs at higher
           baked-in versions than v2.x does, which triggers MSI's "Disallowing installation of component since the 
           same component with higher versioned keyfile exists" rule at CostFinalize. That decision is locked
           in before RemoveExistingProducts can clear the old files. Marking these files CompanionFile of
           morphic_exe makes their install decision inherit from morphic.exe (whose version always
           increases each release), bypassing the per-file version comparison entirely.

           Both DLLs are excluded from heat's harvest by FilterMorphicCoreDll and
           FilterDiaSymReaderNativeAmd64Dll in heatfilter.xsl. They are app-local private DLLs that always
           ship with Morphic.exe, so grouping them in one Component is acceptable here (they have no
           independent lifecycle: not GAC-installed, not COM-registered, not shared with other products).

           If a future build regresses some other file's version, the "Disallowing installation of
           component" message in the install log will identify it and it can be added to this same
           exclude-from-heat + CompanionFile-in-Package.wxs pattern. -->
      <!-- Explicit Guid (vs. the default Guid="*") because WiX 4 requires explicit Guids
           for any Component that contains more than one versioned file, even if the
           non-keypath files are CompanionFiles. Stable across releases so MSI's
           component reference counting tracks correctly across upgrades. -->
      <Component Id="MorphicExeComponent" Guid="2E5F8A1B-3D7C-4E9A-B6F2-1D4C8E7A9B3F">
        <File Id="morphic_exe" Source="Morphic.exe" KeyPath="yes" />
        <File Id="morphic_core_dll" Source="Morphic.Core.dll" CompanionFile="morphic_exe" />
        <!-- DiaSymReader's native DLL has architecture-specific filenames (.amd64 / .arm64).
             The conditional picks the architecture-matched filename for the current build; 
	     the matching FilterDiaSymReaderNative*Dll keys in heatfilter.xsl strip the 
	     heat-generated component for the same file so it doesn't double-declare. -->
        <?if $(sys.BUILDARCH) = "x64" ?>
          <File Id="dia_sym_reader_native_amd64_dll" Source="Microsoft.DiaSymReader.Native.amd64.dll" CompanionFile="morphic_exe" />
        <?elseif $(sys.BUILDARCH) = "arm64" ?>
          <File Id="dia_sym_reader_native_arm64_dll" Source="Microsoft.DiaSymReader.Native.arm64.dll" CompanionFile="morphic_exe" />
        <?endif?>
      </Component>

      <!-- Notification helper EXE + its supporting files. The helper is the COM activator
           that Explorer launches for toast notification clicks; Morphic.exe itself can't
           serve as the activator because its uiAccess=true manifest makes it un-CreateProcess
           able by Explorer (error 740).

           Files are excluded from heat's harvest via heatfilter.xsl's FilterNotificationHelper*
           keys so they're declared here with stable File IDs. The notification_helper_exe
           File ID is referenced by SetUnregisterNotificationHelper's CustomAction Value to
           build the uninstall command line.

           Helper's transitive deps (WindowsAppSDK projection DLLs, .NET runtime files, etc.)
           are NOT declared here. They're shared with Morphic.exe's deps and arrive in the
           install dir via heat's harvest of Morphic's publish output. At runtime, the
           helper's .deps.json-driven resolver finds those shared files in its own directory
           (same dir as helper.exe = same dir as Morphic.exe). Both projects pin the same
           WindowsAppSDK version, so the shared resolution is reliable. This is the entire
           reason both EXEs are designed to live in the same install dir.

           Explicit Guid because the component contains multiple files (WiX 4 requires
           explicit Guid for that). Stable across releases so MSI's component reference
           counting tracks correctly across upgrades. -->
      <Component Id="NotificationHelperComponent" Guid="4A5F8E2B-1D3C-4F7E-A8B9-6C7D8E9F0A1B">
        <File Id="notification_helper_exe" Source="Morphic.NotificationHelper.exe" KeyPath="yes" />
        <File Id="notification_helper_dll" Source="Morphic.NotificationHelper.dll" />
        <File Id="notification_helper_deps_json" Source="Morphic.NotificationHelper.deps.json" />
        <File Id="notification_helper_runtimeconfig_json" Source="Morphic.NotificationHelper.runtimeconfig.json" />
        <File Id="notification_helper_pri" Source="Morphic.NotificationHelper.pri" />
      </Component>
      <ComponentGroupRef Id="MorphicSupportComponents" />
    </ComponentGroup>
  </Fragment>
	
</Wix>
